+    <!-- Roaster's Journal Section -->
+    <section id="roasters-journal" class="journal-section">
+      <div class="journal-header">
+        <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+        <p class="journal-meta">Field Notes &amp; Ethical Sourcing Chronicles | By Head Roaster Marcus Vance</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <img
+        src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1447933601403-0c6688de566e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80"
+        alt="Raw coffee cherries and freshly harvested green beans"
+        class="journal-image"
+      />
+
+      <div id="journal-content" class="journal-content">
+        <p>
+          Our pursuit of the perfect roast begins far beyond the confines of our urban roastery, deep along the cloud-shrouded volcanic ridges of Huila, Colombia and the ancient highland forests of Sidama, Ethiopia. Here, at elevations surpassing 1,800 meters, crisp alpine nights slow down cherry development. This protracted growing cycle compresses vital sugars, producing unusually dense beans packed with complex phosphoric acidity and delicate aromatic precursors. Rather than sourcing through opaque commodity exchanges, we forge direct-trade partnerships with generational smallholder farming families, paying farm-gate prices that average 40% above Fair Trade minimums.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          True sustainability is rooted in living, healthy soil. Our partner cooperatives cultivate exclusively shade-grown heirloom varietals—such as Bourbon, Typica, and Gesha—nestled beneath native acacia and inga canopies. This agroforestry model preserves critical migratory bird corridors, prevents hillside erosion during torrential wet seasons, and naturally eliminates the need for petroleum-based fertilizers. When harvest season arrives between November and February, mechanized stripping is strictly forbidden. Experienced pickers navigate steep terrain on foot, making up to five selective passes across each slope to harvest only deep crimson cherries at peak Brix sugar concentration.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Within three hours of harvest, the cherries arrive at community washing stations where processing begins with natural spring-fed flotation to separate low-density floaters. Depending on the micro-lot's flavor profile, the beans either undergo a clean 36-hour mountain water fermentation or a sealed 72-hour anaerobic natural fermentation in food-grade stainless tanks. From there, the wet parchment is moved to raised African drying beds. Over 18 to 22 days of sun drying, the parchment is raked and turned by hand every thirty minutes from dawn to dusk until internal moisture content stabilizes at a calibrated 10.8% to 11.2%. Every single lot is cupped blindly on-site by certified Q-Graders, and only harvest lots earning an 86-point score or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association (SCA) protocol are approved for shipment.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          To safeguard these fragile aromatic compounds on their transatlantic voyage, our green coffee is sealed in multilayer hermetic GrainPro bags, impervious to ocean humidity and thermal swings. Once the burlap sacks arrive at our roastery floor, we roast strictly to order in 12-kilogram batches inside our vintage cast-iron drum roaster. By meticulously modulating airflow and burner intensity, we coax out each origin's distinctive terroir—from delicate bergamot and jasmine florals to rich, caramelized stone fruit and velvety dark cacao—honoring the countless hands that nurtured the bean from seed to sack.
+        </p>
